--TEST--
`Core\Socket::upgrade`'s `on:` takes `spawn script`'s spelling, and `"worker"` does not compile, because a connection runs on the core that accepted its client: the placement is refused where it is written, never accepted and dropped — `rule:concurrency/an-upgrades-options-are-spawn-scripts`, `rule:concurrency/an-upgrade-is-spawn-shaped`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A misspelled sub-cap is refused as `spawn script` refuses it.
Core\Socket::upgrade("chat.nvs", limits: {memmory: "1M"});
// `"here"` is what an upgrade does, so it compiles.
Core\Socket::upgrade("chat.nvs", on: "here");
Core\Socket::upgrade("chat.nvs", on: "worker");
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0454]: `memmory` is not a limit a spawn site can narrow
  --> case.nvs:3:42
  |
3 | Core\Socket::upgrade("chat.nvs", limits: {memmory: "1M"});
  |                                          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ no sub-cap of this name
  = help: the sub-caps are: `cpu_time`, `max_output`, `memory`, `wall_time`, `max_tasks`

error[E0818]: `Core\Socket::upgrade` cannot run its connection on another core
  --> case.nvs:6:38
  |
6 | Core\Socket::upgrade("chat.nvs", on: "worker");
  |                                      ^^^^^^^^ a connection runs on the core that accepted it
  = help: the client's connection belongs to this core, so the script runs here. Remove `on:`, or write `on: "here"`

error: aborting due to 2 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
